Geminate consonants appear to have existed since the beginning of the Old Irish period, but they were simplified by the end, as is generally reflected by the spelling. Eventually, however, ''ll, mm, nn, rr'' were repurposed to indicate nonlenited variants of those sounds in certain positions.

=== Vowels ===
Written vowels ''a, ai, e, i'' in poststressed syllables (except when absolutely word-final) all seem to represent phonemic {{IPAslink|ə}}. The particular vowel that appears is determined by the quality (broad vs. slender) of the surrounding consonants and has no relation to the etymological vowel quality:
